-- Q1801: Review/Compilation Engagements [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Substantially less in scope than an audit and does not express an opinion","Equivalent in scope to an audit but with different reporting requirements","Limited to confirming account balances and testing internal controls","Designed to detect all instances of fraud and error in the statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AR-C 90.39, the review report must include a statement that a review is substantially less in scope than an audit, the objective of which is expressing an opinion on the financial statements as a whole. This distinguishes limited assurance from reasonable assurance.
Wrong (B): A review is not equivalent in scope to an audit. AR-C 90 clearly differentiates the two levels of service, with reviews providing only limited assurance.
Wrong (C): A review consists primarily of inquiries and analytical procedures, not confirmation of balances or testing controls, which are audit-level procedures.
Wrong (D): A review is not designed to detect all fraud and error. It provides limited assurance through inquiries and analytical procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1801;

-- Q1802: Review/Compilation Engagements [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A statement that the review was performed in accordance with SSARS","A paragraph describing each specific analytical procedure performed","A complete list of all management personnel who were interviewed","A detailed description of each specific inquiry question asked"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AR-C 90.36, the review report for a nonissuer states that the review was conducted in accordance with Statements on Standards for Accounting and Review Services (SSARS) promulgated by the AICPA. The report also identifies the statements reviewed and describes the nature of a review.
Wrong (B): The report does not detail specific analytical procedures performed. It provides a standard description of the review process without listing individual procedures.
Wrong (C): The report does not list management personnel interviewed. Specific personnel details are documented in workpapers, not in the report.
Wrong (D): The report does not disclose specific inquiry questions. It states that the review included inquiries and analytical procedures in general terms.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1802;

-- Q1803: Review/Compilation Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reclassify the debt to current without consulting management first","Discuss the classification with management and consider modifying the report","Ignore the issue since compilations do not provide any assurance","Convert the engagement to a review engagement instead"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AR-C 80.20-.21, when an accountant performing a compilation becomes aware of a misclassification, the accountant should bring the matter to management''s attention and request revision. If management refuses, the accountant should consider whether modification of the compilation report is appropriate to disclose the departure.
Wrong (A): The accountant cannot unilaterally change the financial statements without management''s approval. The statements are management''s responsibility.
Wrong (C): While compilations provide no assurance, the accountant is not permitted to ignore known misclassifications. AR-C 80 requires the accountant to address known departures.
Wrong (D): A misclassification does not require changing the engagement level. The accountant addresses the issue within the compilation engagement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1803;

-- Q1808: Review/Compilation Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Be identical in all respects to an annual review report","Identify the interim information and note management''s responsibility for it","Include a full audit opinion on the interim financial statements","Only be issued if the entity also has an annual audit engagement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AR-C 90, the review report on interim financial statements identifies the interim information reviewed, states that management is responsible for the interim statements, describes the accountant''s review procedures, and provides a limited assurance conclusion tailored to interim financial information.
Wrong (A): While similar to an annual review report, the interim report is specifically tailored to interim financial information and includes language appropriate to interim periods.
Wrong (C): A review report provides limited assurance, not an audit opinion. Audit opinions are issued under AU-C standards, not review engagements under AR-C 90.
Wrong (D): There is no requirement that the entity have an annual audit for the accountant to issue a review report on interim statements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1808;

-- Q1810: Review/Compilation Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["In the title of the review report itself","In the management responsibility section of the report","As a footnote appended at the bottom of the report","In a separate paragraph describing the departure before the conclusion"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AR-C 90.40-.41, when modifying a review report for a known GAAP departure, the accountant includes a separate paragraph describing the departure and its known or estimated effects on the financial statements. This paragraph is placed before the conclusion paragraph to draw attention to the issue.
Wrong (A): The title of the report identifies it as an independent accountant''s review report. The modification is not placed in the title.
Wrong (B): The management responsibility section describes management''s obligations. It is not the appropriate location for describing a GAAP departure.
Wrong (C): Review reports do not use footnotes to communicate modifications. The separate paragraph format is the required presentation under AR-C 90.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1810;

-- Q1813: Review/Compilation Engagements [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Refuse the request because conversion would require audit procedures","Assess whether sufficient information exists to compile on the accrual basis","Only convert if the entity was audited in the prior fiscal year","Issue a disclaimer of opinion on the accrual-basis statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AR-C 80, the accountant can compile financial statements on the accrual basis even if the books are maintained on the cash basis, provided sufficient information is available to make the conversion. The engagement letter should be amended to reflect the accrual-basis framework.
Wrong (A): Converting from cash to accrual basis does not require audit procedures. It involves adjusting the financial statement presentation, which is within the scope of a compilation.
Wrong (C): There is no requirement that the entity have been previously audited. The determining factor is whether sufficient information exists for the accrual-basis conversion.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer is not appropriate here. If sufficient information exists, the accountant compiles the accrual-basis statements; if not, the accountant declines the conversion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1813;

-- Q1814: Review/Compilation Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Comparing the depreciation-to-asset ratio against the prior year and useful lives","Comparing the current-year depreciation expense directly to total revenue","Confirming original asset costs with external vendors and suppliers","Inspecting asset purchase invoices for proper recording and classification"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AR-C 90.32, calculating the ratio of depreciation expense to gross depreciable assets and comparing it to prior years and expected useful lives is an appropriate analytical procedure for a review. It provides insight into whether the average depreciation rate is reasonable and consistent.
Wrong (B): Comparing depreciation to revenue is not a meaningful analytical relationship. Depreciation relates to the asset base, not to revenue generation patterns.
Wrong (C): Confirming costs with vendors is an audit-level procedure involving external confirmations, which goes beyond the scope of review engagement procedures under AR-C 90.
Wrong (D): Inspecting purchase invoices is a detailed test of transactions characteristic of an audit, not the inquiry and analytical procedures used in a review.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1814;

-- Q1815: Review/Compilation Engagements [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the revenue figure since management determined the completion percentage","Recompute all percentage-of-completion calculations independently from source data","Confirm the completion calculations with the entity''s customers directly","Inquire of management about the revenue growth inconsistency with declining backlog"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AR-C 90.36, when analytical procedures reveal an inconsistency such as revenue growing while contract backlog declines, the accountant should inquire of management about the discrepancy. Legitimate explanations may include acceleration of existing contracts or changes in estimation methods.
Wrong (A): Simply accepting management''s figures without inquiry when analytical procedures reveal an inconsistency would not satisfy the accountant''s review responsibilities under AR-C 90.
Wrong (B): Independent recomputation of all calculations is an audit-level procedure that exceeds the scope of a review engagement.
Wrong (C): External confirmations with customers are audit-level procedures, not part of the inquiry and analytical procedures used in a review under AR-C 90.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1815;

-- Q1822: Attestation Engagements [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Based on our review, nothing came to our attention requiring modification","In our opinion, the subject matter is presented fairly in all material respects","We make no representation regarding the subject matter presented","The procedures performed and the findings obtained are as follows"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C 205.57, an unqualified examination opinion uses positive assurance language: "In our opinion, [the subject matter] is presented, in all material respects, based on [the criteria]." This is the highest level of assurance in an attestation engagement.
Wrong (A): Negative assurance language ("nothing came to our attention") is used in review-level engagements under AT-C 210, not examinations.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer stating no representation is made describes a situation where the practitioner cannot form a conclusion, not an unqualified opinion.
Wrong (D): Reporting procedures and findings without an opinion describes an agreed-upon procedures engagement under AT-C 215.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1822;

-- Q1823: Attestation Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Positive assurance expressing an opinion on the subject matter","Negative assurance stating nothing came to attention requiring modification","No assurance with the practitioner merely presenting factual findings","A guarantee that the subject matter is completely accurate"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C 210, a review attestation engagement provides limited (negative) assurance. The conclusion states that based on the review, nothing came to the practitioner''s attention indicating the subject matter is not presented in accordance with the criteria.
Wrong (A): Positive assurance ("In our opinion...") is used in examination engagements under AT-C 205, which provide a higher level of assurance than reviews.
Wrong (C): Presenting findings without assurance describes an agreed-upon procedures engagement under AT-C 215, not a review.
Wrong (D): No attestation engagement provides a guarantee of accuracy. Even examinations provide reasonable, not absolute, assurance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1823;

-- Q1824: Attestation Engagements [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reasonable assurance and an opinion on the subject matter","Limited assurance through negative assurance on the subject matter","No assurance but a report of findings from procedures performed","A compiled presentation of the subject matter information"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AT-C 215.22, in an agreed-upon procedures engagement the practitioner performs only those specific procedures agreed upon by the engaging party and reports the findings without providing any form of assurance. Users draw their own conclusions from the factual findings per AT-C 215.34.
Wrong (A): Reasonable assurance and an opinion are provided in examination engagements under AT-C 205, not agreed-upon procedures engagements.
Wrong (B): Limited (negative) assurance is provided in review engagements under AT-C 210, not agreed-upon procedures engagements.
Wrong (D): A compilation is a separate service under AR-C 80 that involves presenting financial information without assurance, distinct from agreed-upon procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1824;

-- Q1831: Attestation Engagements [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Historical statements were audited or reviewed and adjustments are appropriate","Pro forma adjustments are mathematically correct and assumptions are reasonable","The entity will definitely complete the proposed acquisition transaction","All pro forma statements are prepared in accordance with IFRS standards"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AT-C 310.10, when examining pro forma financial information, the practitioner should obtain satisfaction that the underlying historical financial statements have been audited or reviewed and that the pro forma adjustments give appropriate effect to the significant transaction or event.
Wrong (B): While mathematical accuracy matters, the practitioner''s primary obligation under AT-C 310 is to evaluate the basis of the pro forma information, including the reliability of the underlying historical statements.
Wrong (C): Pro forma information is inherently hypothetical. The practitioner does not assess the certainty of the transaction''s completion.
Wrong (D): Pro forma information may be prepared under any applicable framework, not exclusively IFRS. AT-C 310 does not mandate a specific framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1831;

-- Q1833: Attestation Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue an unqualified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph","Modify the opinion to qualified or adverse based on pervasiveness","Disclaim an opinion on the entity''s compliance entirely","Convert the report to an agreed-upon procedures engagement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C 315, when material noncompliance is identified in a compliance examination, the practitioner must modify the opinion. A qualified opinion is issued if noncompliance is material but not pervasive; an adverse opinion is issued if it is both material and pervasive.
Wrong (A): An emphasis-of-matter paragraph does not adequately address material noncompliance. Material noncompliance requires modification of the opinion itself under AT-C 315.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer is used when the practitioner is unable to obtain sufficient evidence, not when noncompliance has been identified and evaluated.
Wrong (D): Converting to agreed-upon procedures is not an appropriate response to discovered noncompliance. The practitioner addresses findings within the examination framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1833;

-- Q1839: Attestation Engagements [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Suitably supported and provide a reasonable basis for the statements","Guaranteed to produce accurate and verifiable financial results","Identical to assumptions used by industry competitors and peers","Formally approved by the entity''s independent external auditor"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AT-C 305.20, in an examination of prospective financial statements, the practitioner evaluates whether the assumptions provide a reasonable basis for the forecast and are suitably supported by available information. The assumptions must be internally consistent and reflect the responsible party''s plans.
Wrong (B): No examination guarantees accuracy of prospective results. The practitioner assesses reasonableness of assumptions, not certainty of outcomes.
Wrong (C): Competitor assumptions are irrelevant to evaluating the entity''s own forecast. Each entity''s assumptions are evaluated based on its specific circumstances.
Wrong (D): There is no requirement that assumptions be approved by the external auditor. The responsible party develops assumptions; the practitioner evaluates their reasonableness.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1839;

-- Q1840: Attestation Engagements [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The entity''s annual financial statements and related disclosures","The entity''s federal and state income tax return filings","Controls relevant to security, availability, integrity, confidentiality, or privacy","The entity''s revenue recognition policies and related estimates"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): A SOC 2 examination under AT-C 205 evaluates a service organization''s controls relevant to the Trust Services Criteria: security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, and privacy. For Type II, both design and operating effectiveness are tested over the examination period.
Wrong (A): Financial statement audits are governed by AU-C standards, not SOC 2 attestation engagements under AT-C 205.
Wrong (B): Tax return filings are not within the scope of a SOC 2 examination, which focuses on Trust Services Criteria controls.
Wrong (D): Revenue recognition policies are financial reporting matters addressed in financial statement audits, not in SOC 2 examinations of service organization controls.$EXPL$
