-- Q1845: Attestation Engagements [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Report the factual findings including results that may indicate fraud","Investigate the fraud thoroughly before reporting any findings","Ignore the findings since AUP engagements do not involve fraud detection","Issue a formal opinion on the entity''s overall fraud risk level"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AT-C 215, if during an AUP engagement the practitioner discovers findings that may indicate fraud, the practitioner reports those factual findings as part of the procedures performed and considers the effect on the engagement and any communication obligations.
Wrong (B): The practitioner in an AUP engagement is not responsible for conducting a fraud investigation. The role is limited to reporting factual findings from the agreed-upon procedures.
Wrong (C): While AUP engagements are not designed to detect fraud, the practitioner cannot ignore findings that arise from performing the agreed-upon procedures.
Wrong (D): AUP engagements do not involve expressing opinions. The practitioner reports factual findings only, and users draw their own conclusions per AT-C 215.34.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1845;

-- Q4412: Nature and Scope of Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The applicable federal and state tax regulations","Management''s stated preferences and internal policies","The applicable financial reporting framework such as GAAP","The auditor''s own professional standards and guidelines"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 200.11, the auditor''s opinion addresses whether the financial statements are presented fairly, in all material respects, in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework (e.g., U.S. GAAP or IFRS). The framework determines recognition, measurement, and disclosure requirements.
Wrong (A): Tax regulations govern tax reporting, not general-purpose financial statement presentation. The audit opinion references the financial reporting framework.
Wrong (B): Management''s preferences do not determine the standard against which financial statements are evaluated. The applicable framework is established by standard-setters.
Wrong (D): Professional auditing standards govern how the audit is conducted, but the opinion addresses conformity with the financial reporting framework, not with auditing standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4412;

-- Q4420: Nature and Scope of Engagements [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether transactions have been classified in appropriate categories","Whether disclosures are clearly expressed and readily understandable","Whether disclosed events and transactions have actually occurred","Whether all amounts can be traced to underlying source documents"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Tracing amounts to source documents relates to the accuracy and valuation assertion or audit evidence gathering procedures, not to presentation and disclosure. The presentation and disclosure assertions address whether financial information is appropriately presented, classified, described, and disclosed per AU-C 315.
Wrong (A): Classification is a core presentation and disclosure assertion. It addresses whether transactions and events are recorded in the proper accounts and categories.
Wrong (B): Understandability is a presentation and disclosure assertion. It addresses whether disclosures are clearly expressed so users can comprehend the information.
Wrong (C): Occurrence and rights is a presentation and disclosure assertion. It addresses whether disclosed events and transactions actually took place and pertain to the entity.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4420;

-- Q4421: Nature and Scope of Engagements [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The auditor discovered a material misstatement the client refuses to correct","Changed circumstances remove the entity''s need for audited statements","The client is dissatisfied with the preliminary audit findings reported","Management wants to avoid disclosure of a related party transaction"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 210.17, an auditor may agree to change from an audit to a review when there is reasonable justification, such as changed circumstances that remove the requirement for an audit (e.g., a loan covenant no longer requiring audited statements).
Wrong (A): If the auditor has discovered a material misstatement the client refuses to correct, agreeing to downgrade would be inappropriate as it may be an attempt to suppress the finding.
Wrong (C): Client dissatisfaction with findings is not a reasonable justification for changing the engagement. The auditor should not agree to avoid reporting unfavorable results.
Wrong (D): Avoiding related party disclosure is not a legitimate reason to change engagement levels. This would suggest an attempt to conceal required information.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4421;

-- Q4426: Independence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Avoiding circumstances that a reasonable third party would view as compromising","Having no financial interest of any kind in the audit client","Maintaining an unbiased mental attitude throughout the engagement","Following all applicable professional auditing standards completely"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct, independence in appearance means avoiding facts and circumstances that would cause a reasonable and informed third party to conclude the auditor''s integrity, objectivity, or professional skepticism has been compromised. This is distinct from independence in fact.
Wrong (B): Having no financial interest describes one specific independence rule, not the broader concept of independence in appearance. Appearance encompasses all circumstances a third party might question.
Wrong (C): Maintaining an unbiased mental attitude describes independence in fact (independence of mind), not independence in appearance.
Wrong (D): Following auditing standards relates to compliance with professional requirements, not the specific concept of how independence is perceived by third parties.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4426;
